---
import ExerciseLayout from '../layouts/ExerciseLayout.astro';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
---
<ExerciseLayout
  title="Incline Bench Press 1RM Calculator — Estimate Your Incline Max"
  description="Estimate your incline bench press one rep max from a set of 1–12 reps. Six formulas with the range between them, why bench angle matters, and clean-rep rules."
  path="/incline-bench-press-calculator/"
  name="Incline Bench Press Calculator"
  h1="Incline Bench Press One Rep Max (1RM) Calculator"
  lead="Estimate your incline bench press max from a recent set — no max attempt needed. Enter the weight and the clean reps you completed at your usual bench angle."
  exercise="incline-bench"
  lift="Incline bench press"
  example={{ weight: 155, reps: 5, unit: 'lb' }}
  related={[LINKS.bench, LINKS.ohp, LINKS.percent, LINKS.increase]}
  sources={['mayhew', 'lesuer', 'reynolds', 'epley']}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="ib-estimate-heading">
    <h2 id="ib-estimate-heading">How to estimate your incline bench max</h2>
    <p class="answer">
      Press a weight you can lift for 3–6 clean reps on an incline bench, stop when the next rep
      would be a grind or a miss, and enter the weight and reps above. For example, 155 lb × 5
      estimates an incline bench max of roughly 174–185 lb.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 1RM formulas were mostly built and checked on the flat bench press, squat and deadlift
      (Mayhew et al., 1992; LeSuer et al., 1997). The incline press is a similar pressing movement,
      so the estimate is a reasonable guide, but treat it as less certain than a flat bench estimate.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ib-angle-heading">
    <h2 id="ib-angle-heading">Why the bench angle matters</h2>
    <p>
      Incline benches are set anywhere from a low incline to a steep one. The steeper the bench, the
      more the lift shifts towards an overhead press and the less weight most people can lift. An
      estimate only applies to the angle you used, so write the angle down with your sets and keep
      it the same when you compare numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the same reason, do not predict your flat bench from your incline, or the other way round.
      Estimate your flat bench with the <a href="/bench-press-calculator/">bench press calculator</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ib-rep-heading">
    <h2 id="ib-rep-heading">What counts as a clean incline rep</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Touch the upper chest every rep.</strong> Stopping short shortens the range of motion and inflates the estimate.</li>
      <li><strong>No bouncing</strong> off the chest.</li>
      <li><strong>Hips stay on the seat.</strong> Lifting the hips flattens the angle and turns the lift into a flat bench.</li>
      <li><strong>Same grip width</strong> as your heavy sets.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ib-safety-heading">
    <h2 id="ib-safety-heading">Incline bench safety for heavy sets</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a spotter, or an incline bench inside a rack with the safety arms set just below the lowest point of the bar.</li>
      <li>Use a full grip with the thumb wrapped around the bar.</li>
      <li>Check the bench is locked at its angle before you load the bar.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="ib-faq-heading">
    <h2 id="ib-faq-heading">Incline bench max: common questions</h2>
    <h3>How do I calculate my incline bench max by hand?</h3>
    <p>
      Use the Epley formula: <strong>1RM = weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30)</strong>. For 155 lb × 5:
      155 × (1 + 5 ÷ 30) = <strong>180.8 lb</strong>. The calculator runs five more published
      formulas and shows the range between them.
    </p>
    <h3>Does it work for the incline dumbbell press?</h3>
    <p>
      Only to estimate an incline dumbbell max. Enter the weight the way you track it — one dumbbell
      or both together — and the estimate comes out the same way. Do not compare it with a barbell
      incline max.
    </p>
    <h3>How often should I update my incline max?</h3>
    <p>
      Whenever you complete a new hard set of 3–6 reps at the same angle — for many lifters every
      few weeks, or at the end of each training block.
    </p>
  </section>
</ExerciseLayout>
